# Markdown formatting

Follow the [Consensys Markdown formatting guide](https://docs-template.consensys.com/contribute/format-markdown)
for full details.
The rules below cover common conventions in this repository.

## Line length and sentence breaks

- When writing prose, wrap at about 100 characters. Start each sentence on its own line.

## Links

- Use relative links for pages.
- Use descriptive link text.

## Code blocks

- Always add language tags to fenced code blocks.
- Keep examples copy-paste ready.
- Do not include shell prompts in commands.
- Use angle-bracket placeholders for user-provided values.

## Tabs

- Use Docusaurus `<Tabs>` and `<TabItem>` for parallel examples.
- Import `Tabs` and `TabItem` when required by the page.

## Admonitions

- Use Docusaurus admonitions (`:::note`, `:::tip`, `:::warning`, and related types).
- Add a custom title only when it improves scanability.
- Do not nest admonitions.

## Frontmatter

- Required: `description` (single sentence).
- Recommended: `keywords`.
- `sidebar_label` is optional and should only be used when the default nav label is too long.
- If frontmatter includes a `title`, avoid adding a duplicate H1 in page content.

## File and folder names

- Use lowercase and dashes only.

## Diagrams

- Use Mermaid where diagrams are needed and the page already follows that pattern.

## Prettier

A Husky pre-commit hook runs Prettier on staged Markdown, MDX, JavaScript, and JSON.
CI checks documentation Markdown with `npm run format:docs:check`.

Prettier preserves existing line breaks in Markdown and MDX. It often breaks nested
HTML or JSX that contains Markdown lists, including tabs, disclosure elements, and
admonitions. When formatting changes the intended display, wrap that block so Prettier
leaves it alone. Put both comments at the start of the line:

- In `.md` files, use `<!-- prettier-ignore-start -->` and `<!-- prettier-ignore-end -->`.
- In `.mdx` files, use `{/* prettier-ignore-start */}` and `{/* prettier-ignore-end */}`.

Use the form that matches the file type. HTML comments fail MDX compilation, and
Prettier does not recognize JSX comments in Markdown.
